DAY 2: Subnet Mask & Binary Basics - My Understanding So Far (TCM Course) 
Subnet Mask & Binary Basics 

Right now, I am studying subnet masks in the TCM course.
From what I learned, a subnet mask is a 32-bit number that works with an IP address to tell us which part of the IP is the network and which part is the host.
When we do subnetting, there are a few main things we always want to find:

Network address - the first address in the subne
Broadcast address - the last address in the subnet
First available host address - one address after the network address
Last available host address - one address before the broadcast address

We usually work with IPv4 addresses, for example: 192.168.1.0.
An IPv4 address is divided into four numbers, and each number is called an octet.

---

The 8-Bit Octet Chart (What Finally Made Sense)
Each octet is made of 8 bits, which is why we use the 8-bit chart:
128 64 32 16 8 4 2 1
Each bit can be:
1 = ON
0 = OFF

The value of the octet depends on which bits are turned on.

---

Converting an IP Address to Binary
For the IP address 192.168.1.0:
192 in binary:
128 (on) + 64 (on) = 192
Binary: 1100000

168 in binary:
128 (on) + 32 (on) + 8 (on) = 168
Binary: 10101000

1 in binary:
Only the last bit (1) is on
Binary: 00000001

0 in binary:
All bits are off
Binary: 00000000

So the full binary form of 192.168.1.0 becomes:
11000000.10101000.00000001.00000000

---

Subnet Mask in Binary (Network vs Host)
A subnet mask like 255.255.255.0 means:
255 in binary = 11111111
0 in binary = 00000000

So the subnet mask in binary is:
11111111.11111111.11111111.00000000
Here is the key rule that finally clicked for me:
1s represent the network portion
0s represent the host portion

So for:
IP Address:   192.168.1.0
Subnet Mask: 255.255.255.0
The first three octets are network
The last octet is for hosts

